#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087639 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eea68c90-99a8-5254-b799-5f0ed51615a9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Indefinite Integrals Using Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "16b3f250-dfcb-5fa6-bfdb-4acf92d8bd37" :question/math-academy-id "e-765" :question/problem "Starting with the standard Maclaurin result for $e^x$, find the first three terms of the series expansion for $\\displaystyle{\\displaystyle \\int e^{-x^2}\\textrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "The series expansion of $e^{x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x} &= 1 + x + \\frac{1}{2!}x^{2} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-x^2$ in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{-x^{2}} &= 1 + (-x^{2}) + \\frac{1}{2!}(-x^{2})^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 - x^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}x^{4} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, integrating with respect to $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\inte^{-x^{2}}dx &= \\int(1 - x^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}x^{4} + ⋯)dx \\\\\n&= C + x - \\frac{1}{3}x^{3} + \\frac{1}{10}x^{5} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087640 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f133583-cb11-5007-bdef-641838aff5e4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Definite Integrals Using Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "4211b707-bff1-5855-9449-16e14858e6ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-766" :question/problem "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of $\\ln(1+x)$, find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral\n$\\displaystyle{\\displaystyle \\int_0^{1/2}\\ln(1-x^2)\\text{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "The power series expansion of $\\ln(1+x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\ln (1 + x) = x - \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{3}x^{3} + ⋯\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-x^2$ in the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (1 - x^{2}) &= (-x^{2}) - \\frac{1}{2}(-x^{2})^{2} + \\frac{1}{3}(-x^{2})^{3} + ⋯ \\\\\n&=-x^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}x^{4} - \\frac{1}{3}x^{6} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1/2}\\ln (1 - x^{2})dx &= \\int(-x^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}x^{4} - \\frac{1}{3}x^{6} + ⋯)dx \\\\\n&= \\left.(-\\frac{x^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{x^{5}}{5} - \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{x^{7}}{7} + ⋯)\\right|_{0}^{1/2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3 \\cdot 2^{3}} - \\frac{1}{2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 2^{5}} - \\frac{1}{3 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 2^{7}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{24} - \\frac{1}{320} - \\frac{1}{2688} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We can get a numerical approximation by evaluating the first three terms. This gives\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1/2}\\ln (1 - x^{2})dx \\mid \\approx - \\frac{1}{24} - \\frac{1}{320} - \\frac{1}{2688} =-0.04516\n$$\n\nrounded to five decimal places.\n\nThe exact value of the integral, rounded to five decimal places, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1/2}\\ln (1 - x^{2})dx &= -0.04523\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our estimation was pretty good!"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f133583-cb11-5007-bdef-641838aff5e4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Definite Integrals Using Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "860dda14-285e-5ad7-82aa-2698e5c97a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1 - 2x^{3}}dx = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{16} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084561} {:answer/id #uuid "8a517055-c594-50c1-9465-337f036984a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1 - 2x^{3}}dx = 1 - \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{28} - ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084562} {:answer/id #uuid "13c1e798-5b4f-5a7f-b3ec-b6c3b4de50ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1 - 2x^{3}}dx = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{32} + \\frac{1}{224} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084563} {:answer/id #uuid "31c21ecf-014a-59e0-833d-9e75a5a81625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1 - 2x^{3}}dx = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{16} + \\frac{1}{121} - ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084564} {:answer/id #uuid "7b191157-86c7-5fd3-b21d-4ca18bd1db83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1 - 2x^{3}}dx = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{32} + \\frac{1}{144} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084565}] :answer-field/id #uuid "72cd6e5a-2562-5b0e-a9ea-4e4dbfbc3c36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084560}] :question/id #uuid "f631e970-6542-5e8c-b936-21da7739cd3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-103968" :question/problem "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of $\\frac{1}{1 - x}$, find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1 - 2x^{3}}dx$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eea68c90-99a8-5254-b799-5f0ed51615a9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Indefinite Integrals Using Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a579397-1cbf-5516-a8f8-2c51ff44f6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\intxe^{-x^{2}}dx = C + \\frac{1}{5}x - \\frac{1}{10}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{15}x^{3} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084729} {:answer/id #uuid "292922fa-e2d4-58bf-89aa-273b1fee71d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921